WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.707
The expertise reversal effect describes a common instructional design problem:

00:00:03.707 --> 00:00:08.396
support that helps beginners may become unnecessary or inefficient for more advanced learners.

00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:12.003
A novice may need a worked example with step-by-step explanation.

00:00:12.003 --> 00:00:15.609
An experienced learner may find the same explanation slow, repetitive,

00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:21.020
or distracting because they already have the schema that the support is trying to build.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.184
Prior knowledge changes the design need.

00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:26.791
Beginners benefit from more guidance, clearer sequencing, and reduced search.

00:00:26.791 --> 00:00:30.037
Intermediate learners may need partially faded examples, targeted practice,

00:00:30.037 --> 00:00:31.479
and feedback on judgment.

00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:34.365
Advanced learners may need realistic cases, edge conditions,

00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:37.611
and optional references rather than mandatory explanations of basics.

00:00:37.611 --> 00:00:41.218
One design does not serve every learner equally well forever.

00:00:41.218 --> 00:00:43.021
Adaptive design can be simple.

00:00:43.021 --> 00:00:44.103
Offer optional guidance.

00:00:44.103 --> 00:00:46.628
Let learners reveal hints only when needed.

00:00:46.628 --> 00:00:50.596
Provide quick refreshers instead of forcing everyone through a long explanation.

00:00:50.596 --> 00:00:55.285
Use readiness checks to decide whether learners need more examples or more challenge.

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:58.170
Build progression so support fades as competence grows.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.498
These choices respect both working memory limits and the learner's existing knowledge.

00:01:02.498 --> 00:01:07.187
The goal is to support novices without trapping experienced learners in unnecessary detail.

00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:11.876
This matters in workplace learning, where audiences often include people with mixed experience.

00:01:11.876 --> 00:01:16.204
Good design gives beginners a safe path into the material and gives

00:01:16.204 --> 00:01:20.532
experienced learners a way to engage with the parts that actually stretch

00:01:20.532 --> 00:01:21.254
their performance.

00:01:21.254 --> 00:01:24.500
Adaptation keeps effort productive for more of the audience.
